@prefix : <https://w3id.org/cco-gro/onto#> .
@prefix owl: <http://www.w3.org/2002/07/owl#> .
@prefix rdf: <http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#> .
@prefix xml: <http://www.w3.org/XML/1998/namespace> .
@prefix xsd: <http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#> .
@prefix rdfs: <http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#> .
@base <https://w3id.org/cco-gro/onto#> .

<https://w3id.org/cco-gro/onto> rdf:type owl:Ontology ;
                                 owl:versionIRI <https://w3id.org/cco-gro/onto/v1.0> ;
                                 <http://purl.org/dc/elements/1.1/creator> "Umair Arshad" ;
                                 <http://purl.org/dc/elements/1.1/description> """A unified regulatory ontology covering education funding regulations from four jurisdictions (UK, US, Canada, Australia), layered over the Core Compliance Ontology (CCO). Contains 1,295 named classes with 362 cross-jurisdiction 
SKOS alignments."""@en ;
                                 <http://purl.org/dc/elements/1.1/title> "Unified Legislation Regulatory Ontology: Education Funding Regulations Across Four Jurisdictions (United Kingdom, United States, Canada, Australia)"@en ;
                                 <http://purl.org/dc/terms/abstract> "Ontology engineering is a complex, time-consuming, and error-prone process, and is particularly demanding in regulatory domains, where knowledge is expressed through obligations, permissions, prohibitions, conditions, and exceptions that require disciplined representation. Existing LLM-based extraction approaches improve scalability, but often struggle to maintain structural consistency in the generated ontology. We present a schema- and pattern-guided approach for regulatory ontology synthesis, in which LLM extraction is constrained directly within a target ontology rather than mapped to it afterward. The method incorporates human expertise through iterative prompt refinement, SHACL shape authoring, and repair-function design. It operates in two steps: structured decomposition of source text into typed components, followed by ontology generation guided by (i) a foundational schema that provides class and property whitelists, and (ii) ontology design patterns retrieved through embedding similarity to support structural alignment. A layered validation architecture combines SHACL conformance checking with SHACL-informed deterministic repair to address property-domain and type-discipline errors introduced during generation. We evaluate the approach through a multi-stage framework covering quality metrics, ontology correctness, and source-text faithfulness. The method is demonstrated on education funding regulations across four jurisdictions, showing that schema- and pattern-constrained generation supports the synthesis of structurally consistent regulatory ontologies." ;
                                 <http://purl.org/dc/terms/bibliographicCitation> "Arshad, U., Corsar, D., Nkisi-Orji, I. (2026). Synthesising Regulatory Ontologies: A Schema and Pattern-Guided Approach" ;
                                 <http://purl.org/dc/terms/contributor> "David Corsar" ,
                                                                        "Ikechukwu Nkisi-Orji" ;
                                 <http://purl.org/dc/terms/issued> "2026-05-23" ;
                                 <http://purl.org/dc/terms/license> <https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/> ;
                                 <http://purl.org/dc/terms/rights> "CC-BY 4.0"@en ;
                                 <http://purl.org/dc/terms/source> "https://github.com/RGU-Computing/Synthesising-Regulatory-Ontologies" ;
                                 owl:versionInfo "1.0" .

